 ## Background
-The concept of implicit surfaces dates back to constructive solid geometry, but neural representations emerged with the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) [S2, unofficial]. Subsequent works like DeepSDF and NeuS adapted this to explicit surface representations using SDFs [S1][S2, unofficial]. MetaNLR++ and NeuMesh represent recent advancements focusing on efficient training and explicit surface editing [S1]. These methods are widely used in novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and geometric modeling [S1][S2, unofficial].
+The concept of implicit surfaces dates back to constructive solid geometry, but neural implicit representations emerged with the advent of deep learning. DeepSDF introduced the use of autoencoders to learn continuous signed distance functions for shape representation [S1]. Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) popularized volumetric neural rendering, but subsequent works like NeuS and MetaNLR++ focused on explicit surface extraction via SDFs to improve geometry quality and rendering speed [S1][S1]. RBF-based methods have long been used in fluid simulation visualization, with recent work integrating them into unbiased path tracing frameworks [S1].
 
